# Test: an operator's `request plugin restart <name>` over SSH stops a RUNNING
#       external (forked, connect-back) plugin and brings a new process of it
#       back running, and the answer says a running plugin was stopped.
# Spec: spec-doctor-coverage-12-plugin-infra R-11
# VALIDATES: handlePluginRestart
#   (internal/component/plugin/server/register_plugin_restart.go) reads
#   was-running from the slot's process, then restartPlugin
#   (server/reload_tx.go) runs ProcessManager.Respawn
#   (plugin/process/manager.go), which stops and joins the forked process and
#   forks its replacement, and restartHandshake (server/restart.go) brings the
#   replacement to StageRunning before the answer. applyFailurePolicy
#   (server/failure_policy.go) applies no policy to the stopped generation,
#   because the slot already holds the replacement, so the operator's restart
#   raises neither doctor-plugin-crashed nor doctor-plugin-stopped. An unknown
#   name is refused (pluginRestartRefusal) with a nonzero exit.
# PREVENTS: a restart answered "stopped and started again" while the forked
#   process was never replaced, or replaced by one that never serves; and an
#   operator's restart reported as a crash or a stop.
# Why not a unit test: the unit tests drive the handler with internal
#   plugins. Only the daemon route proves the command reaches the handler over
#   SSH and that a forked plugin's process is really replaced.
#
# No `ze init`: the store is not part of the question. The daemon starts from
# the file on the runner's default in-memory store and authenticates the
# config's own user; the client takes its credentials and the daemon's port
# from the environment (resolveUsername, resolvePassword, resolveHostPort,
# internal/component/cli/sshclient/client.go), exactly as
# test/plugin/doctor-plugin-stopped.ci does, which carries the full reasoning.
#
# The fixture, plugin/restart-generation
# (internal/test/fixture/plugin_fixture_restart_generation.go), serves one
# command, `show restart-generation status`, until the daemon closes its
# connection, and answers restart-generation-<N>: N counts the processes that
# answered, kept in a marker file in the plugin's working directory because a
# restart is a new process. That count is what tells the replacement from the
# process it replaced; the product prints no plugin pid on any CLI command
# (`show plugin list` lists the binary's registry, not processes).

option=timeout:value=60s
option=env:var=ze.ssh.username:value=admin
option=env:var=ze.ssh.password:value=testpass
option=env:var=ze.ssh.port:value=$PORT
tmpfs=running.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF
system {
	authentication {
		# bcrypt cost-4 hash of "testpass".
		user admin {
			password "$2a$04$UlwuiuH82Unfsq.XEMPGJeDkXwbm3KW.nvVaVXOd/JeFK8VjMjrQO"
		}
	}
}
environment {
	ssh {
		enabled true
		server main {
			ip 127.0.0.1;
			port $PORT;
		}
	}
}
plugin {
	external restart-generation {
		run "le test fixture plugin/restart-generation"
		encoder json
	}
}
EOF_CONF

# "Ze running." is printed after the standalone SSH server's Start returned,
# which is after its listener reached Accept (serveListener,
# internal/component/ssh/listener_start.go), and after
# WaitForStartupComplete, as test/plugin/doctor-plugin-stopped.ci relies on.
cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze start running.conf:name=daemon:ready=Ze running.

# Barrier, state 1: the first generation runs and serves. The daemon
# dispatching the plugin's own command to it, and the plugin answering, is the
# state "running" means to an operator; it also makes this process count
# itself as generation 1 before the restart (the count is taken at the first
# answer). daemon/await-answer retries until the command exits zero with the
# needle, so a refused connection or a not-yet-registered command is "not
# yet", never a failure.
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=le test fixture daemon/await-answer restart-generation-1 "show restart-generation status":exit=0

# The command under test. Its answer is the handler's Data map: message,
# plugin, was-running (fieldMessage, fieldRestartedPlugin,
# fieldRestartWasRunning). was-running is true because the slot's process
# reported Running() when the handler read it, before Respawn.
cmd=foreground:seq=3:exec=ze cli -c "request plugin restart restart-generation | json":exit=0
expect=stdout:contains=plugin restart-generation was running: stopped and started again
expect=stdout:pattern="was-running":\s*true
expect=stdout:pattern="plugin":\s*"restart-generation"

# A new process serves now, with no barrier: restartHandshake returns only once
# the replacement reached StageRunning, and handlePluginRestart answers after
# it, so a plain dispatch right after the answer must reach the replacement.
# Generation 2 is a second process counting itself; generation 1 answering
# here would mean the old process still serves.
cmd=foreground:seq=4:exec=ze cli -c "show restart-generation status":exit=0
expect=stdout:contains=restart-generation-2
reject=stdout:contains=restart-generation-1

# The operator's restart is not a crash and leaves no stop record.
# ReportCrash (doctor-plugin-crashed, an error on the bus) and RecordStopped
# (doctor-plugin-stopped, a warning) are both reached only past
# applyFailurePolicy's slot check, which returns because the name holds the
# replacement: Respawn stores it under pm.mu, the same lock GetProcess takes.
# Ordering: restartPluginProcess releases the old registrations, which waits
# for the old generation's handleSingleProcessCommandsRPC cleanup, before the
# answer; the old superviseProcess then runs applyFailurePolicy with no further
# wait. No product output marks that call's return, so these rejects are
# ordered against the RPC loop's end, not against applyFailurePolicy itself.
cmd=foreground:seq=5:exec=ze cli -c "show errors | json":exit=0
reject=stdout:contains=doctor-plugin-crashed
cmd=foreground:seq=6:exec=ze cli -c "show warnings | json":exit=0
reject=stdout:contains=doctor-plugin-stopped

# The one refusal an operator reaches here: a name nothing configured.
# pluginRestartRefusal answers an opererr through OperatorErrorResponse
# (Status error, Error set); the standalone SSH executor's
# CommandDispatcher.Answer (internal/component/plugin/dispatch.go) turns that
# into an error, so the exec channel exits nonzero and `ze cli` prints the
# error on stderr, as test/ui/show-config-running.ci asserts for its refusal.
cmd=foreground:seq=7:exec=ze cli -c "request plugin restart no-such-plugin":exit=1
expect=stderr:contains=plugin no-such-plugin is not a configured plugin
expect=stderr:contains=only a plugin the configuration or its auto-load started can be restarted
expect=stderr:contains=ze show doctor, which names every configured plugin
